Supplementary Table 8. Pharmacological implication of the coincident associations.	Unnamed: 1	Unnamed: 2	Unnamed: 3	Unnamed: 4	Unnamed: 5	Unnamed: 6	Unnamed: 7	Unnamed: 8	Unnamed: 9	Unnamed: 10	Unnamed: 11	Unnamed: 12	Unnamed: 13
A. Results about the pharmacological implication of the coincident associations													
Coincident associations reported in Supplementary table 5A have been screened for optimal candidates for pharmaceutical development. Only disease association studies with p-value< 1x10-6 are considered.													
eGENES are from Supplementary Table S4A.													
HLA and SH2B3 regions were excluded due to high-level of pleiotropy. 													
"In the fifth  and sixth columns, ""comma"" indicates the simultaneous need for specific markers, “or” the mutually exclusive presence of two markers, and a slash symbolizes the presence of at least one of the two indicated markers. "													
In the sixth column, protein targets are indicated in blue.													
"In the column ""eGenes"", expression traits (eGenes) are separated by :::: and expression traits can contain multiple eGenes (e.g. the signal is referred to a region where genes are overlapping). + and - indicate respectively the increment or decrement of the expression trait with the risk allele for the patology indicated in the Disease column. The presence of both + and - for the same expression trait indicate that there is at least a study reporting - and a study reporting + effect for the risk allele."													
AC=absolute count, RC=relative count													
The disease acronyms are those of Figure 3.													
													
													
